"CSS Box Alignment Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-align-3/)\nciteturn1605view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-align-3/#baseline-align-content\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1179\nL0: # CSS Box Alignment Module Level 3\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Editor’s Draft†www.w3.org】, 8 October 2026\nL3: \nL4: More details about this document\nL5: This version:\nL6:     【1†https://drafts.csswg.org/css-align/】\nL7: Latest published version:\nL8:     【2†https://www.w3.org/TR/css-align-3/†www.w3.org】\nL9: Feedback:\nL10:     【3†CSSWG Issues Repository†github.com】\nL11:     【4†Inline In Spec】\nL12: Editors:\nL13:     【5†Elika J. Etemad / fantasai†fantasai.inkedblade.net】 (Apple)\nL14:     【6†Tab Atkins Jr.†xanthir.com】 (Google)\nL15: Suggest an Edit for this Spec:\nL16:     【7†GitHub Editor†github.com】\nL17: 【8†Copyright†www.w3.org】 © 2026 【9†World Wide Web Consortium†www.w3.org】. W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)^{®} 【10†liability†www.w3.org】, 【11†trademark†www.w3.org】 and 【12†permissive document license†www.w3.org】 rules apply.\nL18: \nL19: * * *\nL20: ## Abstract\nL21: \nL22: This module contains the features of 【13†CSS†www.w3.org】 relating to the alignment of boxes within their containers in the various CSS box layout models: block layout, table layout, flex layout, and grid layout. (The alignment of text and inline-level content is defined in 【14†[CSS-TEXT-3]】 and 【15†[CSS-INLINE-3]】.)\nL23: \nL24: 【13†CSS†www.w3.org】 is a language for describing the rendering of structured documents (such as HTML and XML) on screen, on paper, etc.\nL25: ## Status of this document\nL26: \nL27: This is a public copy of the editors’ draft. It is provided for discussion only and may change at any moment. Its publication here does not imply endorsement of its contents by W3C. Don’t cite this document other than as work in progress.\nL28: Please send feedback by 【16†filing issues in GitHub†github.com】 (preferred), including the spec code “css-align” in the title, like this: “[css-align] …summary of comment…”. All issues and comments are 【17†archived†lists.w3.org】. Alternately, feedback can be sent to the (【18†archived†lists.w3.org】) public mailing list www-style@w3.org.\nL29: \nL30: This document is governed by the 【19†18 August 2025 W3C Process Document†www.w3.org】.\nL31: \nL32: The following features are at-risk, and may be dropped during the CR period:\nL33:   * [ first | last ]? baseline\nL34:   * the 【20†<overflow-position>】 keywords\nL35:   * the scrollable-area safety trigger into 【21†safe】 mode when no 【20†<overflow-position>】 is specified\nL36:   * the 【22†legacy】 keyword for 【23†justify-items】\nL37:   * the effect of the 【24†box alignment properties】 on absolutely-positioned boxes with 【25†auto】 offsets\nL38: “At-risk” is a W3C Process term-of-art, and does not necessarily imply that the feature is in danger of being dropped or delayed. It means that the WG believes the feature may have difficulty being interoperably implemented in a timely manner, and marking it as such allows the WG to drop the feature if necessary when transitioning to the Proposed Rec stage, without having to publish a new Candidate Rec without the feature first.\nL39: ## Table of Contents\nL40:   1. 【26†1 Introduction】\nL41:     1. 【27†1.1 Module Interactions】\nL42:     2. 【28†1.2 Value Definitions】\nL43:     3. 【29†1.3 Partial Implementations】\nL44:   2. 【30†2 Overview of Alignment Properties】\nL45:   3. 【31†3 Alignment Terminology】\nL46:   4. 【32†4 Alignment Keywords】\nL47:     1. 【33†4.1 Positional Alignment: the center, start, end, self-start, self-end, flex-start, flex-end, left, and right keywords】\nL48:     2. 【34†4.2 Baseline Alignment: the baseline keyword and first/last modifiers】\nL49:     3. 【35†4.3 Distributed Alignment: the stretch, space-between, space-around, and space-evenly keywords】\nL50:     4. 【36†4.4 Overflow Alignment: the safe and unsafe keywords and scroll safety limits】\nL51:       1. 【37†4.4.1 Automatic Overflow Alignment Safety】\nL52:         1. 【38†4.4.1.1 Content Distribution for Scroll Containers】\nL53:         2. 【39†4.4.1.2 Self-Alignment for Absolutely Positioned Boxes】\nL54:         3. 【40†4.4.1.3 All Other Alignment】\nL55:   5. 【41†5 Content Distribution: Aligning a Box’s Contents Within Itself】\nL56:     1. 【42†5.1 The justify-content and align-content Properties】\nL57:       1. 【43†5.1.1 Block Containers (Including Table Cells)】\nL58:       2. 【44†5.1.2 Multicol Containers】\nL59:       3. 【45†5.1.3 Flex Containers】\nL60:       4. 【46†5.1.4 Grid Containers】\nL61:     2. 【47†5.2 Content-Distribution Shorthand: the place-content property】\nL62:     3. 【48†5.3 Alignment Overflow and Scroll Containers】\nL63:     4. 【49†5.4 Baseline Content-Alignment】\nL64:   6. 【50†6 Self-Alignment: Aligning the Box Within Its Parent】\nL65:     1. 【51†6.1 Inline-Axis (or Main-Axis) Self-Alignment: the justify-self property】\nL66:       1. 【52†6.1.1 Block-Level Boxes】\nL67:       2. 【53†6.1.2 Absolutely-Positioned Boxes】\nL68:       3. 【54†6.1.3 Table Cells】\nL69:       4. 【55†6.1.4 Flex Items】\nL70:       5. 【56†6.1.5 Grid Items】\nL71:     2. 【57†6.2 Block-Axis (or Cross-Axis) Self-Alignment: the align-self property】\nL72:       1. 【58†6.2.1 Block-Level Boxes】\nL73:       2. 【59†6.2.2 Absolutely-Positioned Boxes】\nL74:       3. 【60†6.2.3 Table Cells】\nL75:       4. 【61†6.2.4 Flex Items】\nL76:       5. 【62†6.2.5 Grid Items】\nL77:     3. 【63†6.3 Self-Alignment Shorthand: the place-self property】\nL78:     4. 【64†6.4 Baseline Self-Alignment】\nL79:     5. 【65†6.5 Effects on Sizing of Absolutely Positioned Boxes with Static-Position Insets】\nL80:   7. 【66†7 Default Alignment】\nL81:     1. 【67†7.1 Inline-Axis (or Main-Axis) Default Alignment: the justify-items property】\nL82:     2. 【68†7.2 Block-Axis (or Cross-Axis) Default Alignment: the align-items property】\nL83:     3. 【69†7.3 Default Alignment Shorthand: the place-items property】\nL84:   8. 【70†8 Gaps Between Boxes】\nL85:   9. 【71†9 Baseline Alignment Details】\nL86:     1. 【72†9.1 Determining the Baselines of a Box】\nL87:     2. 【73†9.2 Baseline Alignment Grouping】\nL88:     3. 【74†9.3 Aligning Boxes by Baseline】\nL89:   10. 【75†Appendix A: Static Position Terminology】\nL90:   11. 【76†10 Changes】\nL91:   12. 【77†11 Privacy Considerations】\nL92:   13. 【78†12 Security Considerations】\nL93:   14. 【79†Acknowledgments】\nL94:   15. 【80†Conformance】\nL95:     1. 【81†Document conventions】\nL96:     2. 【82†Conformance classes】\nL97:     3. 【83†Partial implementations】\nL98:       1. 【84†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features】\nL99:     4. 【85†Non-experimental implementations】\nL100:   16. 【86†Index】\nL101:     1. 【87†Terms defined by this specification】\nL102:     2. 【88†Terms defined by reference】\nL103:   17. 【89†References】\nL104:     1. 【90†Normative References】\nL105:     2. 【91†Non-Normative References】\nL106:   18. 【92†Property Index】\nL107:   19. 【4†Issues Index】\nL108: ## 1. Introduction\nL109: \nL110: CSS Levels 1 and 2 allowed for the alignment of text via 【93†text-align】 and the alignment of blocks by balancing auto margins. However, except in table cells, vertical alignment was not possible. As CSS adds further capabilities, the ability to align boxes in various dimensions becomes more critical. This module attempts to create a cohesive and common box alignment model to share among all of CSS.\nL111: Note: The alignment of text and inline-level content is defined in 【14†[CSS-TEXT-3]】 and 【15†[CSS-INLINE-3]】.\nL112: \nL113: Note: This specification is not intended to change any of the behavior defined in 【94†CSS2.1†www.w3.org】 when the properties defined here are set to their initial values. If implementors or anyone else notices a discrepancy, please report this to the CSSWG as an error.\nL114: \nL115: This section (above) is not normative.\nL116: ### 1.1. Module Interactions\nL117: \nL118: This module adds some new alignment capabilities to the block layout model described in 【95†[CSS2]】 【96†chapters 9†www.w3.org】 and 【97†10†www.w3.org】, 【52†redefines how overconstrained block-level box margins are resolved】, and defines the interaction of these new alignment properties with the alignment of table cell content using 【98†vertical-align】, as defined in 【95†[CSS2]】 【99†chapter 17†www.w3.org】.\nL119: The interaction of these properties with Grid Layout 【100†[CSS-GRID-1]】 and Flexible Box Layout 【101†[CSS-FLEXBOX-1]】 is defined in their respective modules. The property definitions here supersede those in 【101†[CSS-FLEXBOX-1]】 (which have a smaller, earlier subset of permissible values).\nL120: \nL121: No properties in this module apply to the `::first-line` or `::first-letter` pseudo-elements.\nL122: ### 1.2. Value Definitions\nL123: \nL124: This specification follows the 【102†CSS property definition conventions†www.w3.org】 from 【95†[CSS2]】 using the 【103†value definition syntax†www.w3.org】 from 【104†[CSS-VALUES-3]】. Value types not defined in this specification are defined in CSS Values & Units 【104†[CSS-VALUES-3]】. Combination with other CSS modules may expand the definitions of these value types.\nL125: In addition to the property-specific values listed in their definitions, all properties defined in this specification also accept the 【105†CSS-wide keywords】 as their property value. For readability they have not been repeated explicitly.\nL126: ### 1.3. Partial Implementations\nL127: \nL128: Since it is expected that support for the features in this module will be deployed in stages corresponding to the various layout models affected, it is hereby clarified that the 【106†rules for partial implementations†www.w3.org】 that require treating as invalid any unsupported feature apply to any alignment keyword which is not supported across all layout modules to which it applies for layout models in which the implementation supports the property in general.\nL129: For example, if an implementation supports 【107†align-self】 in 【100†[CSS-GRID-1]】 and 【101†[CSS-FLEXBOX-1]】, then it must treat 【108†start】 as invalid unless it is supported in both grid and flex containers. However if that same implementation does not support align-self for block-level elements at all, then a lack of implementation of align-self: start does not trigger this requirement to treat it as invalid.\nL130: ## 2. Overview of Alignment Properties\nL131: \nL132: The box alignment properties in CSS are a set of 6 properties that control alignment of boxes within other boxes. They can be described along two axises:\nL133: \nL134:   * which dimension they apply to (【109†main】/【110†inline】 vs. 【111†cross】/【112†block】), and\nL135:   * whether they control the position of the box within its parent, or the box’s content within itself.\nL136: Note: This specification uses the terms “justify” and “align” to distinguish between alignment in the main/inline and cross/block dimensions, respectively. The choice is somewhat arbitrary, but having the two terms allows for a consistent naming scheme that works across all of CSS’s layout models (including 【113†CSS Flexbox 1 § 2 Flex Layout Box Model and Terminology】)\nL137: \nL138: The following table summarizes the 【24†box alignment properties】 and the display types they can apply to.\nL139: Common  | Axis  | Aligns  | Applies to\nL140: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL141: 【114†justify-content】  | main/inline  | 【115†Image】 content within element (effectively adjusts padding)  | 【116†block containers】, 【117†flex containers】, and 【118†grid containers】\nL142: 【119†align-content】  | cross/block\nL143: 【120†justify-self】  | inline  | 【121†Image】 element within parent (effectively adjusts margins)  | block-level boxes, absolutely-positioned boxes, and 【122†grid items】\nL144: 【107†align-self】  | cross/block  | absolutely-positioned boxes, 【123†flex items】, and 【122†grid items】\nL145: 【23†justify-items】  | inline  | 【124†Image】 items inside box (controls child items’ justify-self: auto)  | 【116†block containers】 and 【118†grid containers】\nL146: 【125†align-items】  | cross/block  | 【117†flex containers】 and 【118†grid containers】\nL147: Note: The *-items properties don’t affect the element itself. When set on a container, they specify the interpretation of any *-self: auto used on children of the container element.\nL148: ## 3. Alignment Terminology\nL149: \nL150: Since this module defines alignment properties for all layout modes in CSS, some abstract terminology is introduced:\nL151: alignment subject\nL152:     The 【126†alignment subject】 is the thing or things being aligned by the property. For 【120†justify-self】 and 【107†align-self】, the alignment subject is the margin box of the box the property is set on, and assumes the 【127†writing mode】 of that box. For 【114†justify-content】 and 【119†align-content】, the alignment subject is defined by the layout mode and refers to some aspect of its contents; it also assumes the writing mode of the box the property is set on.\nL153: alignment container\nL154:     The 【128†alignment container】 is the rectangle that the 【126†alignment subject】 is aligned within. This is defined by the layout mode, but is usually the alignment subject’s containing block, and assumes the 【127†writing mode】 of the box establishing the containing block.\nL155: fallback alignment\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/)\nciteturn1605view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/#numeric-types\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2959\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Flexible Box Layout Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-flexbox-1/)\nciteturn1605view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-flexbox-1/#cross-sizing\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3589\n"
